
.TH DECLAN 1 "July 2026" "Declan" "User Commands"



.SH NAME
Declan \- Utility to manage Arch-based Linux declaratively.



.SH SYNOPSIS
.B declan
.IR < operation > " [option]"



.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Declan
is a utility to declaratively manage Arch and Arch-based Linux systems.
.br
Its main inspiration is NixOS's 'configuration.nix'. However, Declan's configuration is merely declarative, and thus not programmable nor extensible.




.SH CONFIGURATION
Declan reads a JSON file which records the user's configuration preferences. The file is initialized, and must remain located at the root of the user's home directory. All of its capabilities are showcased in the example below:

.nf
{
    "description": "<user>'s <distro> configuration",

    "packages": [],
  
    "services": [],
    
    "gc": {
        "enabled": true,
        "include": [
            "~/*"
        ]
    },

    "rice": {
        "enabled": false,
        "repo": "<user>/<repo>",
        "include": [
            "<app>/"
        ]
    },

    "backup": {
        "enabled": true,
        "path": "~/<name>.tar.xz",
        "include": [
            "~/"
        ]
    }
}
.fi

The file structure displayed herein is mandatory, and it is automatically set upon initialization.
.br
By default, nothing is listed or enabled. Users can choose any combination of features.


.SS Item Formatting

The 'packages', 'services', and 'include' keys have array values. Following JSON's syntax, items in those arrays must be "quoted", mandatorily in double quotes. If there are multiple items in an array, all but the last should be appended with a comma (,), like so:

.nf
"key": [
    "foo",
    "bar",
    "baz"
]
.fi

.br
In the array of packages, list packages as they're named in their repository (extra/AUR/etc.). Metapackages are supported.
.sp 1
In the array of services, list services as they're named in the /systemd/system/ directories. The extension '.service' can be omitted.
.sp 1
All three 'include' keys take paths, though formatting varies:
.nf
    - In 'gc' and 'backup', tilde expansion (~) (to the user's home directory) is supported.
    - In 'gc', the globbing pattern '*' is supported.
    - In 'rice', paths are appended to ~/.config/, and should point directly to files and directories within it without any extra leading characters.  
.sp 1
Though unnecessary, consider appending paths which are directories with a slash (/) for self-documentation.




.SH EXTRAS
Since Declan only reads its pre-defined keys, the user can take advantage of this by creating other, unrelated keys, which declare things that Declan does not manage.
.sp 1
For example, new keys can be created inside existing ones:

.nf
"backup": {
    "enabled": true,
    "path": "~/Backup.tar.xz",

    "remove_later": "~/temp"
    "include": [
        "~/temp",
        "~/.local/share",
        ...
    ]
}
.fi

New top-level keys can also be helpful:

.nf
"reminders": {
    "scheduler": "scx_rustland",
    
    "scripts": [
        "ln -s ~/.config/file ~/file",
        "chsh -s /usr/bin/zsh"
    ]
}
.fi

Automated script execution is not a planned feature in Declan 1.x. However, external tools can parse Declan's configuration file if the feature is desired.




.SH OPERATIONS
Configurations are applied to the system through Declan's various command-line operations:

.TP
.BR 'init'
Initializes Declan through a series of command-line prompts.
.IP
Behavior: Searches for a previous initialization log. If not found, shows the logo screen and begins initialization. Looks in Declan's cache to try to find a previous configuration file. If found, asks the user whether to read it, and whether to keep it (this prompt is shown before any operation if the environment variable is missing). Requests a name for the configuration file. Creates (or copies to, depending on whether a configuration was kept) the file in the current user's home root (~/), and logs its creation. If a file of the same name already exists, skips creation. Finds the user's login shell, writes an environment variable export to its startup file, with the value of the configuration's path ($DECLAN_CONFIG_PATH). Exits after showing additional instructions.

.TP
.BR 'clear'
Remove Declan's data from the system.
.IP
Behavior: Warns the user of deletion risks. Asks for initial confirmation. Sequentially prompts the user on whether to delete cached files, erase the environment variable, and delete the configuration file, safely skipping each if no approval is given.

.TP
.BR 'relay'
Install and enable newly added packages and services, remove and disable delisted ones. Options: --casc;
.IP
Behavior: If 'yay' is not installed, prompts the user to do so. Loads the cached configuration file if it exists. Shows the list of packages to install (in the current list but not in the previous one), and to remove (in only the previous one). The same is done for services to enable and disable. If all the lists are empty, warns the user and exits. Installs listed packages as explicit, so that declared packages are never removed unless taken out of the list. Removes delisted packages and their dependencies. Note: if the '--casc' option was passed, cascading removal (removing everything that depends on each package) will be attempted; Pacman's package removal prompt will be shown for each package, and if the user does not approve, the package is removed normally. Services are enabled and disabled immediately. The current configuration file is cached with a timestamp in Declan's cache, and the previous cached configuration is deleted.

.TP
.BR 'rebuild'
Perform a system upgrade and enable services. Options: --casc, --gc
.IP
Behavior: Same as 'relay', but lists all packages pending updates to their latest version, and executes the upgrade. Note: upgrades all packages, even if not declared in the configuration. Alongside '--casc', it also permits the '--gc' flag, which automatically chains its execution with 'gc'.

.TP
.BR 'gc'
Remove listed files and directories, orphaned packages, and package cache.
.IP
Behavior: Reads the list of paths declared in the configuration file. Removes all listed files and directories. WARNING: deletion is not reversible. Removes orphaned packages and package cache. Note: the latter has its own multi-line prompt.

.TP
.BR 'rice'
Upload select configuration files to a remote repository. Options: --get
.IP
Behavior: If the GitHub CLI is not installed, prompts the user to do so. WARNING: only GitHub repositories are supported. Checks the user's GitHub authentication status. If a repository is not yet initialized at ~/.config, does so. WARNING: 'rice' won't function if a repository is present in ~/.config, remove existing ones beforehand. Commits only the listed items with a message recording the time, and uploads to the listed repository. The output of 'git push' is shown. The '--get' flag quickly clones the repository into ~/.config/declan-rice.

.TP
.BR 'backup'
Back up listed files and directories to an xz-compressed tarball.
.IP
Behavior: If 'pv' (Pipe Viewer) is not installed, prompts the user to do so. Note: always uses 'xz'. Requests the desired compression level. Calculates the total size to compress (in gigabytes) and shows the backup's progress bar. Note: compression is parallelized. WARNING: leading slashes are removed, so files will not be extracted directly into the system root. Note: backups preserve files' full paths.
.IP
The 'path' key requires including the file extension: '.tar.xz', and will result in broken backups without it.




.SH OPTIONS
.IP
The options '--help' and '--version' (and their aliases) are to be used on their own without an operation. '--gc' and '--casc' should be used with their supported operations; if both are used, their order is irrelevant.

.TP
.BR -h ", " --help
Show command-line usage information.

.TP
.BR -v ", " --version
Show the program's logo, current version, and license terms.

.TP
.B --gc
Perform garbage collection after 'rebuild' finishes.

.TP
.B --casc
Iteratively attempt cascading removal of packages set for uninstallation. See 'relay' for details.

.TP
.B --get
Copies files from the specified repository into ~/.config/declan-rice




.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
declan -v
declan --help
declan rebuild --gc --casc
declan backup
.fi
